Configure and test Network Interfaces
===============================================================
Goal: Find the network interface, set a static IP + gateway with
nmcli, set hostname, set DNS, add a secondary IP, then test with
ping and check /etc/resolv.conf.

Values used in the practical (change them if your lab uses others):
  Interface  : ens33
  Primary IP : 192.168.1.50/24
  Gateway    : 192.168.1.1
  Hostname   : webserver01.lab.local
  DNS        : 8.8.8.8 and 8.8.4.4
  Second IP  : 192.168.1.51/24

NOTE: The connection name is usually the same as the interface
name (ens33). Check with:  nmcli connection show


STEP 1: Identify the network interface using ip addr
----------------------------------------------------
$ ip addr show

Look for the interface after "lo" (here: ens33). Note its current
IP (here: 192.168.152.128/24, dynamic).


STEP 2: Set static IP and gateway using nmcli
---------------------------------------------
$ sudo nmcli connection modify ens33 ipv4.addresses 192.168.1.50/24
$ sudo nmcli connection modify ens33 ipv4.gateway 192.168.1.1
$ sudo nmcli connection modify ens33 ipv4.method manual

(ipv4.method manual is required, otherwise the static IP is
 not used and the connection stays on DHCP.)


STEP 3: Set hostname
--------------------
$ sudo hostnamectl set-hostname 'webserver01.lab.local'

Verify:
$ hostnamectl
(Static hostname: webserver01.lab.local)


STEP 4: Configure DNS server
----------------------------
$ sudo nmcli connection modify ens33 ipv4.dns '8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4'

Apply the changes (restart the connection):
$ sudo nmcli connection down ens33 && sudo nmcli connection up ens33


STEP 5: Add a secondary IP to the same interface
------------------------------------------------
$ sudo nmcli connection modify ens33 +ipv4.addresses 192.168.1.51/24
$ sudo nmcli connection up ens33

(The "+" before ipv4.addresses ADDS an address. Without it the
 old address is replaced.)

Confirm both IPs are present:
$ ip addr show ens33


STEP 6: Test ping gateway, ping by name, check /etc/resolv.conf
---------------------------------------------------------------
By gateway:
$ ping -c 4 192.168.1.1

By name:
$ ping -c 4 webserver01.lab.local

Check /etc/resolv.conf:
$ cat /etc/resolv.conf

Expected content:
  # Generated by NetworkManager
  search lab.local
  nameserver 8.8.8.8
  nameserver 8.8.4.4

(You can also open it with: sudo nano /etc/resolv.conf)


NOTE ABOUT THE SCREENSHOTS IN YOUR DOC
--------------------------------------
Your "By Gateway" screenshot pings 192.168.1.50, which is the
machine's OWN IP, not the gateway. To test the gateway, ping
192.168.1.1 as shown above. If the gateway does not exist in your
VMware network, the ping will fail, and that is expected.


